{"title":"2012 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_25\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 25</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_25\">Problem 25</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 25\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/3/903aad7ecd1bf5e4dd8c556473d5894c4c166e3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\{(x,y) : x\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}, y\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\},\\text{ and } (x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"545\" height=\"18\" />. \nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of all right triangles whose vertices are in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. For every right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/f/9bfaec0c14fc8da404cce61af690664f738551a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t=\\triangle{ABC}$\" width=\"89\" height=\"13\" /> with vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> in counter-clockwise order and right angle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/e/3def3d21ca4449b1990e61e0163aea58bf358f74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(t)=\\tan(\\angle{CBA})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"18\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/7/557dedb14b9ddd5d2ef6b6c8ff8260739c362351.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\prod_{t\\in T} f(t)?\\]\" width=\"66\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/7/a479cb3ed3c6f87f54058d88565b9a4a9527e3df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 1\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{625}{144}\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{125}{24}\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 6\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{625}{24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"418\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider reflections. For any right triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> with the right labeling described in the problem, any reflection <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/2/022948689ac03ea1705a6af78b86d32ae2a369eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> labeled that way will give us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/3/113d94a32df5c3a9e31ce38df659aec6191ae6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan CBA \\cdot \\tan C&#39;B&#39;A&#39; = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"203\" height=\"14\" />. First we consider the reflection about the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/f/9cf93643d66b00e6ac489e35ecf1e29c83df230a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=2.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" />. Only those triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b45e24fd5bdbdcd91ad8e228df8967475cc09f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\subseteq T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> that have one vertex at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/e/11e8da5448a5f378d5b8c253cdcd3cb49a90fa49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> do not reflect to a traingle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b45e24fd5bdbdcd91ad8e228df8967475cc09f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\subseteq T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" />. Within those triangles, consider a reflection about the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/8/e68741d15d63d34c3dc3d88a9d003e855a4b9e37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=5-x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" />. Then only those triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b45e24fd5bdbdcd91ad8e228df8967475cc09f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\subseteq T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> that have one vertex on the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> do not reflect to a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b45e24fd5bdbdcd91ad8e228df8967475cc09f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\subseteq T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" />. So we only need to look at right triangles that have vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/5/fe51e8b05cc9e9271ba8e86d53be1748da1dcf87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,5), (*,0), (*,*)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />. There are three cases:\n</p><p>Case 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/b/66b7b001d61d5240d7a8eda64b20ffb77f786c6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=(0,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/c/d5cc7b65a744ad8d9e95e9e2cbb37aabcb3d2123.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=(*,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> is impossible.\n</p><p>Case 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/0/710cb6c49a1daf824059dce86e0f3054333f9ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=(0,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />. Then we look for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/8/f18cd7ca4a29cd012ac0aca9bd7372d22f7d605f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/9/5c99c9788acceb02c2c365409a0b2bd56c56fa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAC=90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"103\" height=\"13\" /> and that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/7/477d200cca685f711f0917eadba419647fd2fbaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=(*,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />. They are: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/a/6da95ba604023aeaf255e1c83ea0d1d36f9e40a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A=(x,5), C=(x,0))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"180\" height=\"18\" />,  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/e/36e228824bc99381fc94f96c53a67093787fdf79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A=(3,2), C=(1,0))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/0/9a0afee355b6f23cea14a4133590ff83e6d5e8d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A=(4,1), C=(3,0))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"18\" />. The product of their values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6e891094852435c420c723456e2a0df6039ef75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan \\angle CBA$\" width=\"84\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/9/c89d776eb2b038adc1a932e1e816826102c6c35b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{5}{1}\\cdot  \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{625}{144}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"198\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Case 3: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/f/50fc1e719c987e5c4c16f8565b44c6082e9710d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C=(0,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c8637e153edf6bb84fca1a9aded9f78614653dd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=(*,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" /> is impossible.\n</p><p>Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/1/2d1b88fa82dc8dda85d0b46f697ba10a4081395f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}  \\ \\frac{625}{144}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"77\" height=\"48\" /> is the answer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This is just another way for the reasoning of solution 1. Picture the question to be a grid of unit squares instead of a coordinate system. Note that the restriction, (This is just to make visualization easier.) Define a \"cell\" to be a rectangle in the set of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/7/0d7e49ad93a99ed6dcafd1c6acba511a181ab6a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> For example, a cell can be (labeled in a red):\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/b/feb2d3e889d88937a06a07ad75d5785f5ba6a775.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5 cm); draw((0,1)--(0,5),black); draw((1,0)--(1,5),black); draw((2,0)--(2,5),black); draw((3,0)--(3,5),black); draw((4,0)--(4,5),black); draw((0,1)--(5,1),black); draw((5,0)--(5,5),black); draw((1,0)--(5,0),black); draw((0,2)--(5,2),black); draw((0,3)--(5,3),black); draw((0,4)--(5,4),black); draw((0,5)--(5,5),black); draw((0,1)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(0,5),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),red); draw((5,5)--(5,1),red); [/asy]\" width=\"122\" height=\"122\" />\n</p><p>Note that choosing any three points and labeling them according to the problem will result in a product of one. For example, with the cell we just labeled, the four triangles we can create are:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/c/becb3347bb575a21d5b5a0b493fcb2c8c7a233a1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5 cm); draw((0,1)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(0,5),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),red); draw((5,5)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(5,1),black); draw((0,1)--(0,5),black); draw((0,5)--(5,1),black);  pair A, B, C;  A = (0,1); B = (0,5); C = (5,1); [/asy]\" width=\"122\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/3/b833951bf372b8aa5be026b3a9fe73d603df8a48.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5 cm); draw((0,1)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(0,5),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),red); draw((5,5)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(5,1),black); draw((5,1)--(5,5),black); draw((5,5)--(0,1),black); [/asy]\" width=\"122\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb5cdb8395743d0be9b6beac26e2e4cb84b40180.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5 cm); draw((0,1)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(0,5),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),red); draw((5,5)--(5,1),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),black); draw((5,1)--(5,5),black); draw((0,5)--(5,1),black); [/asy]\" width=\"122\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/7/547af90637d40923d1f980b1155db3340ca5f1fe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(0.5 cm); draw((0,1)--(5,1),red); draw((0,1)--(0,5),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),red); draw((5,5)--(5,1),red); draw((0,5)--(5,5),black); draw((0,1)--(0,5),black); draw((0,1)--(5,5),black); [/asy]\" width=\"122\" height=\"98\" />\n</p><p>If we define the longer side to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and the shorter side to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d1d002db36c51a77f8fbc6314e96756fc793e28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" /> then the product will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/c/31c2da149ec54cf7baff5efce0cf22798531b42b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x}=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"137\" height=\"38\" /> and we are done.\n</p><p>Otherwise, the three points are not contained in a \"cell.\" This will result in the solution 1 path as described before. Our three points must take the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6b48126f5ab1617bff5e1756a590b3df317a7b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,5), (*,0), (*,*),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/4/1e4bb5c0261c8d726157108cdd95cf6eb7bb6d46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$*$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\" /> is a number defined by the boundaries of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/7/0d7e49ad93a99ed6dcafd1c6acba511a181ab6a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> Thus, by the three cases, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/0/0903e4aee7969d5c3bb94560a66d68b6689d8564.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}  \\ \\frac{625}{144}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"83\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~wesserwes7254\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2012_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2012amc12b/279\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2012amc12b/279</